Hitchhiking is legal in the United Kingdom and no law forbids asking a stranger for a lift; motorways are the exception. [https://www.gov.uk/guidance/the-highway-code/motorways-253-to-273 Rule 253 of the Highway Code] states that motorways must not be used by pedestrians, citing the Motorways Traffic (England and Wales) Regulations 1982 (regs 3(d), 4 and 11) and, in Scotland, the Motorways Traffic (Scotland) Regulations 1995 — the ban covers the carriageway, the hard shoulder and the slip roads. Rule 271 adds that nobody may stop on a carriageway, emergency area, hard shoulder, slip road, central reservation or verge except in an emergency, so a lift cannot lawfully be picked up on the motorway itself. Motorway service areas are private land reached off the motorway rather than part of it, so asking drivers there is lawful, though the operator can ask you to leave. On A-roads and everything below them, stand safely off the carriageway and thumb: there is no offence in it.
